<%# S3a T4 — the assistant's companion lane (plan §7.1, §7.7, L11/L12).

    Rendered ONCE at body level (like _personal_tasks_drawer and the search
    overlay, and for the same reason: the sidebar's transform/overflow would clip
    a position:fixed child). The outer node carries a stable id plus
    data-turbo-permanent, so Turbo transplants the LIVING element into every page
    it visits: no re-render, no server roundtrip, no flicker. Typed draft, scroll
    position and the loaded transcript all stay put across navigation.

    STATE THAT MUST SURVIVE BELONGS ON THE NODE, not in the controller instance
    — the node is what data-turbo-permanent guarantees. Stimulus values give us
    exactly that for free (they live in data attributes on this element).

    Three of the four entry points from §7.1 are wired here (the fourth, the
    context button at an object, is S4):
      1. the sidebar row (outside this controller's scope -> custom event),
      2. Cmd/Ctrl+J from anywhere,
      3. the capsule, i.e. the panel's own collapsed state.

    Server-rendered open/width come from the cookie (AssistantPanelState) so a
    hard reload paints the correct geometry immediately instead of jumping once
    the controller connects. %>
<% if assistant_available? %>
  <% panel_state = assistant_panel_state %>
  <div id="assistant_panel"
       data-turbo-permanent
       data-controller="assistant-panel"
       data-assistant-panel-src-value="<%= organisation_assistant_panel_path(organisation_id: current_organisation.id) %>"
       data-assistant-panel-open-value="<%= panel_state.open? %>"
       data-assistant-panel-width-value="<%= panel_state.width %>"
       data-assistant-panel-min-width-value="<%= AssistantPanelState::MIN_WIDTH %>"
       data-assistant-panel-max-width-value="<%= AssistantPanelState::MAX_WIDTH %>"
       data-assistant-panel-sidebar-width-value="<%= AssistantPanelState::SIDEBAR_WIDTH %>"
       data-assistant-panel-cookie-name-value="<%= AssistantPanelState::COOKIE_NAME %>"
       data-assistant-panel-mode-value="<%= panel_state.mode %>"
       data-assistant-panel-artefact-src-value="<%= organisation_assistant_artefact_path(organisation_id: current_organisation.id) %>"
       data-assistant-panel-artefact-value=""
       data-assistant-panel-thread-url-value="<%= organisation_assistant_chats_path(organisation_id: current_organisation.id) %>"
       data-assistant-panel-panel-mode-value="<%= AssistantPanelState::MODE_PANEL %>"
       data-assistant-panel-full-mode-value="<%= AssistantPanelState::MODE_FULL %>"
       data-assistant-panel-open-class="assistant-panel-open"
       data-assistant-panel-full-class="assistant-panel-full"
       class="hidden lg:block">

    <button type="button" class="mobile-assistant-scrim" data-action="assistant-panel#close" tabindex="-1" aria-label="<%= t('mobile.close') %>"></button>
    <%# The panel proper. start-64 docks it against the sidebar; the width is set
        by the controller from the node state (the inline value here is only the
        first paint). %>
    <%# assistant-panel is the hook for components/assistant_panel.css; the full
        view is a CLASS on this same element, never a second element and never a
        page of its own (L11). The inline width is the first paint of the
        companion mode only -- in the full view the CSS ignores it, because the
        width belongs to the layout there (§7.7).

        ⚠️ The outer edge is deliberately QUIETER than the house style
        (27.08.2026, on sight): a 2px black border plus a hard 6px shadow made the
        panel read as a wall against the page it accompanies. It now uses the same
        1px cream-300 line its own lanes use inside (assistant_panel.css), plus a
        soft shadow that only says "this floats" -- the panel travels WITH the app,
        so it should not look bolted onto it. %>
    <div data-assistant-panel-target="panel" aria-label="<%= t('navigation.assistant') %>"
         data-action="keydown->assistant-panel#closeOnEscape keydown->assistant-panel#trapMobileFocus"
         style="<%= "width: #{panel_state.width}px" unless panel_state.full? %>"
         class="assistant-panel <%= 'assistant-panel--full' if panel_state.full? %> <%= 'hidden' unless panel_state.open? %> fixed inset-y-0 start-64 z-drawer flex flex-col bg-surface-raised border-e border-cream-300 shadow-[4px_0_12px_-6px_rgba(0,0,0,0.25)]">

      <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 px-4 py-3 border-b-2 border-cream-300 shrink-0">
        <h2 class="min-w-0 text-sm font-bold text-cream-900 flex items-center gap-2">
          <i class="bi bi-stars shrink-0"></i>
          <span class="truncate"><%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.title') %></span>
        </h2>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-1 shrink-0">
          <%# S3c T2: up to the full view and back down (§4). TWO buttons rather
              than one that rewrites its own label: this header sits in a
              permanent node, so the server can never correct it, and a button
              whose accessible name is patched by JavaScript is the kind of thing
              that ends up announcing the wrong action. Each button says one
              thing, the controller only decides which of them is visible. %>
          <button type="button"
                  data-assistant-panel-target="expandButton"
                  data-action="assistant-panel#expandToFull"
                  class="<%= 'hidden' if panel_state.full? %> w-7 h-7 flex items-center justify-center text-cream-500 hover:text-cream-900 hover:bg-cream-200 rounded transition-all"
                  title="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.expand') %>"
                  aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.expand') %>">
            <i class="bi bi-arrows-angle-expand text-sm"></i>
          </button>
          <button type="button"
                  data-assistant-panel-target="collapseButton"
                  data-action="assistant-panel#collapseToPanel"
                  class="<%= 'hidden' unless panel_state.full? %> w-7 h-7 flex items-center justify-center text-cream-500 hover:text-cream-900 hover:bg-cream-200 rounded transition-all"
                  title="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.collapse') %>"
                  aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.collapse') %>">
            <i class="bi bi-arrows-angle-contract text-sm"></i>
          </button>
          <%# NOT a link, unlike its neighbour: this one needs to know which
              thread the frame is showing so the list can mark it and offer the
              way back, and a permanent node never learns that (the server can
              never re-render it). The controller reads it off the transcript. %>
          <button type="button"
                  data-assistant-panel-target="historyButton"
                  data-action="assistant-panel#showHistory"
                  title="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.history.open') %>"
                  aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.history.open') %>"
                  class="<%= 'hidden' if panel_state.full? %> w-7 h-7 flex items-center justify-center text-cream-500 hover:text-cream-900 hover:bg-cream-200 rounded transition-all">
            <i class="bi bi-clock-history text-sm"></i>
          </button>
          <%# The gear, for the same reason the history button next to it is a
              button and not a link: the way back out of the settings has to land
              on the conversation this was pressed on, and only the browser knows
              which one that is (the header is permanent, the server never
              re-renders it). A plain link would return to the panel default, i.e.
              the newest thread, and switch the conversation under somebody who
              pressed a button twice. %>
          <button type="button"
                  data-assistant-panel-target="settingsButton"
                  data-action="assistant-panel#showSettings"
                  title="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.settings.open') %>"
                  aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.settings.open') %>"
                  class="w-7 h-7 flex items-center justify-center text-cream-500 hover:text-cream-900 hover:bg-cream-200 rounded transition-all">
            <i class="bi bi-gear text-sm"></i>
          </button>
          <%# Targets the frame from OUTSIDE it: the header is part of the
              permanent shell, only the body below is exchanged. %>
          <%# The one ACTION in a header of otherwise quiet controls, so it wears the
              primary token rather than the faint treatment of its neighbours.
              Deliberately WITHOUT the design system's lift shadow: the header row is
              28px tall and a 3px offset shadow would collide with the border below it.

              ⚠️ And deliberately BELOW the house style's border-2 black (27.08.2026,
              on sight): in this header it was the only bordered surface at all, which
              made a 28px button shout louder than the panel title. A hairline in a
              muted tone plus a softened fill keeps it recognisable as the action; the
              full token comes back on hover, where the pointer already says the same
              thing. %>
          <%= link_to organisation_assistant_panel_path(organisation_id: current_organisation.id, new: 1),
                      data: { turbo_frame: 'assistant_panel_body' },
                      title: t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.new_chat'),
                      aria: { label: t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.new_chat') },
                      class: 'w-7 h-7 flex items-center justify-center border border-stroke/30 rounded ' \
                             'bg-btn-primary/60 text-ink hover:bg-btn-primary transition-all' do %>
            <i class="bi bi-plus-lg text-sm"></i>
          <% end %>
          <button type="button"
                  data-action="assistant-panel#close"
                  title="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.close') %>"
                  aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.close') %>"
                  class="w-7 h-7 flex items-center justify-center text-cream-500 hover:text-cream-900 hover:bg-cream-200 rounded transition-all">
            <i class="bi bi-x-lg text-sm"></i>
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%# The lanes of §7.2, as a grid INSIDE the panel rather than the page-level
          .layout grid: that one lives in <main>, gets its height from
          layout_toggle_controller and is addressed via `main:has(.layout)`, none
          of which a fixed body-level element can join. In the companion mode the
          grid has ONE track (the chat) and the history lane is display:none; the
          full view adds the lane back. Rules in components/assistant_panel.css.

          The consequence that matters: switching modes is pure CSS. The chat
          frame is never re-fetched, so a typed draft, the scroll position and a
          streaming answer all survive the way up and back down -- which is the
          whole reason the full view is a state of this node and not a page. %>
      <div class="assistant-lanes flex-1 min-h-0">
        <%# The history lane: same component as the overlay list from T1, loaded
            into a frame of its OWN. Lazy like the body -- somebody who never
            expands never pays for it. %>
        <%= turbo_frame_tag 'assistant_panel_history',
                            class: 'assistant-lane-history min-h-0',
                            data: { assistant_panel_target: 'historyFrame' } do %>
          <p class="p-4 text-sm text-cream-500"><%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.loading') %></p>
        <% end %>

        <%# No src attribute: the controller sets one on first open (from its own
            src value above), so a user who never opens the panel never pays for the
            transcript. Deliberately NOT a second copy of the address in a data-src
            here -- the controller never read it, and the same URL in two places is
            the kind of pair that drifts. %>
        <%= turbo_frame_tag 'assistant_panel_body',
                            class: 'assistant-lane-chat min-h-0 flex flex-col',
                            data: { assistant_panel_target: 'frame' } do %>
          <p class="p-4 text-sm text-cream-500"><%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.loading') %></p>
        <% end %>

        <%# The form the "Assistent" button in an object's header ends up
            submitting (Bezugs-Sammlung S1, §8.3). It lives HERE and not on the
            page for the same reason the panel is a permanent node: the page
            cannot know which thread the panel is showing, and the address of this
            write carries that thread's id. The controller fills in both the
            action and the key before it submits.

            POST to .../assistant_chats/:id/references since S1: adding a
            reference is a write to a COLLECTION, not a patch of a column, and
            the field carries the composite key ("Ticket:<uuid>") that is the same
            value everywhere (§7.1).

            A real form and not fetch(): Turbo then applies the returned stream
            the way it applies every other one. ⚠️ What carries the CSRF check is
            the GLOBAL token Turbo sends as X-CSRF-Token from csrf_meta_tags, not
            the hidden field form_with mints here: per_form_csrf_tokens binds that
            field to the address rendered HERE, and the controller rewrites the
            action before submitting. So this form must stay a Turbo submission.
            Same move as the composer submit the capsule performs. %>
        <%= form_with url: organisation_assistant_chats_path(organisation_id: current_organisation.id),
                      method: :post, class: 'hidden',
                      data: { assistant_panel_target: 'contextForm' } do %>
          <%= hidden_field_tag 'key', '', id: nil,
                               data: { assistant_panel_target: 'contextField' } %>
        <% end %>

        <%# The artefact lane (S3c T3, §4, §8.1): empty and hidden until something
            opens it, then exchanged like any frame.

            ⚠️ target="_top" belongs HERE, on the shell's frame, not on the frame
            in the response: Turbo keeps this element and only swaps its children,
            so attributes the server sends on the response frame never arrive.
            Without the escape every link inside a panel (a row's billing badge,
            the way out into the time list) would load a whole page INTO this
            frame and render "Content missing" -- the same trap the citation list
            documents. The controller answers such a navigation by stepping down
            to the companion mode, so the page it opens is actually visible. %>
        <%= turbo_frame_tag 'assistant_panel_artefact',
                            target: '_top',
                            class: 'assistant-lane-artefact min-h-0',
                            data: { assistant_panel_target: 'artefactFrame' } do %>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%# The context offer used to sit HERE, a strip pinned to the panel's
          bottom edge. It moved into the chat frame on 27.08.2026
          (Neo::Chat::ContextOfferComponent), above the row that shows the
          reference it offers to set: down here, under the field, the style row
          and the transparency line, it was where nobody looked. What kept it in
          this shell was that a frame load wipes anything the client wrote into
          the frame -- answered at the load itself, in handleChatFrameLoad. %>
      <%# Resize handle straddling the panel's right edge. Focusable and driven by
          the arrow keys as well as the pointer: role="separator" is a promise,
          and a separator that only answers a mouse does not keep it. %>
      <div data-action="pointerdown->assistant-panel#startResize keydown->assistant-panel#resizeByKey"
           data-assistant-panel-target="resizeHandle"
           role="separator"
           tabindex="0"
           aria-orientation="vertical"
           aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.resize') %>"
           aria-valuemin="<%= AssistantPanelState::MIN_WIDTH %>"
           aria-valuemax="<%= AssistantPanelState::MAX_WIDTH %>"
           aria-valuenow="<%= panel_state.width %>"
           class="<%= 'hidden' if panel_state.full? %> absolute inset-y-0 -end-1 w-2 cursor-col-resize hover:bg-cream-300 focus:bg-cream-300 focus:outline-none"></div>
    </div>

    <%# The capsule: the panel's COLLAPSED state, not a second thing. It hovers
        where the panel would be; typing plus Enter unfolds the panel in place and
        hands the text over. While the page scrolls it shrinks to the icon so it
        stops covering the last row; scrolling up, hover or a click bring it back,
        and it never shrinks while somebody is typing in it. %>
    <div data-assistant-panel-target="capsule"
         class="<%= 'hidden' if panel_state.open? %> fixed bottom-4 start-72 z-drawer w-64 focus-within:w-96 transition-all duration-200">
      <input type="text"
             autocomplete="off"
             data-assistant-panel-target="capsuleInput"
             data-action="keydown.enter->assistant-panel#submitCapsule"
             placeholder="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.capsule_placeholder') %>"
             aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.capsule_label') %>"
             class="w-full text-sm border-2 border-black rounded-full px-4 py-2 bg-surface-raised text-cream-900 shadow-[3px_3px_0_0_#000] placeholder:text-cream-500">
      <button type="button"
              data-assistant-panel-target="capsuleMini"
              data-action="click->assistant-panel#openFromMini mouseenter->assistant-panel#expandCapsule"
              title="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.capsule_label') %>"
              aria-label="<%= t('views.organisations.assistant_panels.shell.capsule_label') %>"
              class="hidden w-10 h-10 rounded-full border-2 border-black bg-surface-raised shadow-[3px_3px_0_0_#000] items-center justify-center text-cream-900">
        <i class="bi bi-stars"></i>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>
